    "knowledge_point": "Obtaining a Higher-Order Derivative Of a Function Given its Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial",
    "problem": "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=-3+5(x+2)-7(x+2)^{2}+9(x+2)^{3}-11(x+2)^{4}+13(x+2)^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(5)}(-2)?$",
    "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x+2)^{5}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(-2)}{5!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(5)}(-2)}{5!} & =13 \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(5)}(-2)}{120} & =13 \\\\ {f}^{(5)}(-2) & =1\\,560.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
